    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Pizarro founded Lima in 1535 in a valley that the Ichma, the Wari and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> had held, and in 1543 it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>.</li><li>It grew rich in the 17th century on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> trade through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>, then lost ground to earthquakes, new viceroyalties and the Bourbon Reforms, and in 1821 it became the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</li><li>Migration from the Andes from the 1940s made it one of the largest cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, and it is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, holding about a third of the country's people in its metropolitan area.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name Lima probably comes from Limaq, the name of a famous oracle in the Rímac valley that the Spanish destroyed and replaced with a church.</p></aside>
    </section>

