    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1535</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> founds the City of Kings in the Rímac valley</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1536</span><span class="rf-text">Troops sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> besiege the city and are defeated by the Spanish and their indigenous allies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1543</span><span class="rf-text">Lima becomes the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> and the seat of a Real <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1687</span><span class="rf-text">Earthquakes on 20 October and 2 December destroy most of the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1746</span><span class="rf-text">An earthquake severely damages Lima and destroys <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1821</span><span class="rf-text">The viceroy leaves, and the city council signs a Declaration of Independence at the request of San Martín</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1879</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> begins, and Chilean troops later occupy and loot the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1940</span><span class="rf-text">An earthquake strikes, and rapid growth from migration from the Andes begins</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachacamac.jpg" alt="Pachacamac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pachacamac_-_51163366566.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apollo, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lima lies on the desert coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, in the valleys of the Chillón, Rímac and Lurín rivers, overlooking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. The city began with its Spanish foundation in 1535, but the valleys were occupied long before. Pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> settlements there were grouped under the Lordship of Ichma, and the Maranga and Lima cultures established an identity in these territories. The Wari Empire conquered these cultures, and the ceremonial centre of Cajamarquilla was built during that time. As Wari importance declined, local cultures regained autonomy, notably the Chancay culture, and in the 15th century the territory was incorporated into the Inca Empire. The Ichma left some 40 pyramids tied to the irrigation system of the valleys.</p><p>Before the Spanish arrived, a famous oracle in the Rímac valley was known as Limaq, which means "talker" or "speaker" in the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> that was the area's main language. The Spanish destroyed the oracle and replaced it with a church, but the name persisted, and scholars think "Lima" is the Spanish pronunciation of Limaq. In Inca times the valley was highly populated and formed a province called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a>. The part of it near the Rímac was administered by a local lord, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuraka">curaca</a> named Taulichusco, who had been awarded it for his services to the Inca royalty. His residential palace stood where the Government Palace stands today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lima rose quickly after its foundation. In 1541 it became the seat of a Roman Catholic diocese, in 1543 the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, and in 1551 it gained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-university-of-san-marcos">National University of San Marcos</a>. Its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press followed in 1584. The city flourished in the 17th century as the centre of a trade network that joined the viceroyalty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the Far East. Its merchants sent Peruvian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> through the nearby port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> and exchanged it for imported goods at the trade fair of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portobelo">Portobelo</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, and law required all the viceroyalty's overseas trade to pass through Callao. The population grew from about 25,000 in 1619 to an estimated 80,000 in 1687.</p><p>The decline came after 1687, when an earthquake coincided with a recession in trade, a fall in silver production and competition from cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. In the second half of the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a> took away the city's monopoly on overseas trade, and the mining region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> passed to the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. Revenue from guano exports revived growth in the 1850s, and the state paid for large public buildings such as the Central Market and the Hospital Dos de Mayo. A railway to Callao was completed in 1850, and the iron Balta Bridge opened in 1870. The population passed 100,000 in 1861 and had doubled by 1927.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro.jpg" alt="Francisco Pizarro" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Francisco_Pizarro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amable-Paul Coutan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before 1535 the valleys passed from the Lordship of Ichma to the Wari Empire, then back to local rule, and in the 15th century to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>. In 1532 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and his Spanish and indigenous allies took the Inca ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> prisoner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>. Atahualpa was sentenced to death although a ransom had been paid, and the Spanish went on to conquer his empire. The Spanish Crown named Pizarro governor of the lands he had conquered, and he founded Lima as his capital after a failed attempt to establish one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jauja">Jauja</a>. In 1543 Lima became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, and the viceroys governed from it. The creation of the Viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> in 1717 and of the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> in 1776 reduced the territory it governed.</p><p>In the 1810s the city was a Royalist stronghold under the viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-fernando-de-abascal">José Fernando de Abascal</a> y Sousa. A Patriot expedition under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> landed south of Lima in 1820. Faced with a naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> action, the viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-la-serna">José de la Serna</a> evacuated the city in July 1821, and San Martín proclaimed the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Lima became the seat of his government and of the first Constituent Congress, and the capital of the Republic of Peru. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> (1879 to 1883) Chilean troops occupied the city after the battles of San Juan and Miraflores.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Lima&#8221; cites where it covers Precolonial, Colonial period, Republican era:</p><ul><li>James Higgins, Lima: A Cultural History (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rose-of-lima"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rose-of-lima.jpg" alt="Rose of Lima" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rose-of-lima">Rose of Lima</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sta_Rosa_de_Lima_por_Claudio_Coello.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claudio Coello, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pizarro, with Nicolás de Ribera, Diego de Agüero and Francisco Quintero, traced the Plaza Mayor and the street grid, and the first settlement had 117 housing blocks. In 1562 another district was built across the Rímac, and the first stone bridge dates from 1610. At that time Lima had about 26,000 people, of whom around 40 per cent were black and around 38 per cent white. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afro-peruvians">Afro-Peruvians</a> of today descend from Africans first brought to the region as slaves. The city was the residence of Spanish nobles in large houses and palaces and of many religious orders, and in the 17th century it became a city of monastic life, home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rose-of-lima">Saint Rose of Lima</a> and Saint Martín de Porres. The image of Christ called the Lord of Miracles has been carried in procession every October since 1746, and the city council named it patron of the city in 1715.</p><p>In the late 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideas of public health led to a cockfighting coliseum, the Acho bullring and a General Cemetery, which ended burial in churches. Chinese and fewer Japanese immigrants arrived in the 19th and early 20th centuries and settled in Barrios Altos, and other immigrants founded their own schools. The Spanish of Lima was shaped by the Castilian nobility of the viceroyalty and later by Italian, African, Chinese, Japanese and Andean influences. The newspaper El Comercio was founded in 1839.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manco-inca.jpg" alt="Manco Inca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SAHUARAURA_p108_MANCO_INCA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In August 1536 the new city was besieged by troops sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a>, who was leading a rebellion against Spanish rule. After heavy fighting in the streets and around the city, the Spanish and their indigenous allies, among them the Huaylas army under Contarhuancho, defeated them. Earthquakes destroyed a large part of Lima between 1586 and 1687, and those of 20 October and 2 December 1687 destroyed most of the city. Disease and food shortages followed, and the population fell to under 40,000 by 1692. On 28 October 1746 another earthquake severely damaged the city and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>, and Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-manso-de-velasco">José Antonio Manso de Velasco</a> directed the rebuilding.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">Pirates</a> and privateers also threatened Lima. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet under Jacques l'Hermite attacked Callao in 1624 and was repelled, English buccaneers were routed by Lima merchants in 1690, and the city walls were built between 1684 and 1687. During the wars of independence the city changed hands several times in two years and suffered exactions from both sides. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>, after the battles of San Juan and Miraflores, Chilean troops looted museums, libraries and schools, and angry mobs attacked wealthy citizens and the Asian community and sacked their businesses. An earthquake struck on 24 May 1940, when most of the city was built of adobe and quincha. In the 1980s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-revolutionary-movement">Túpac Amaru Revolutionary Movement</a> were centred in Lima, and terrorist violence added to the displacement of people and the decay of the historic centre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Historic Centre of Lima was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1988. Its colonial buildings include the Basilica and Convent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, the Metropolitan Cathedral and the Palacio de Torre Tagle, in styles drawn from Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoclassicism">Neoclassicism</a>, and it holds over 1,600 balconies from the viceregal and republican periods. Pre-Hispanic huacas such as Huallamarca, Pucllana and Mateo Salado still stand among the business and residential buildings of districts that have grown around them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-university-of-san-marcos">National University of San Marcos</a>, founded in 1551, is the oldest continuously operating university in the New World, and the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was founded in 1821.</p><p>The rapid growth that began in the 1940s came from migration from the Andean regions. The population, estimated at 600,000 in 1940, reached 1.9 million by 1960 and 4.8 million by 1980. Migrants raised shanty towns known as barriadas, later called pueblos jóvenes, through large land invasions. Lima is now the capital and largest city of Peru. A 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> projection gives the city about 10 million people, and around a third of the country's population lives in its metropolitan area. Its cooking mixes Spanish, Andean, African, Chinese and Japanese traditions, from the immigrants and migrants who settled there.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/hanacpachap-cussicuinin">Hanacpachap cussicuinin<span>1631</span></a><a href="/songs/el-condor-pasa">El Cóndor Pasa<span>1913</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Lima, in the valleys of the Chillón, Rímac and Lurín rivers on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> in 1535 in a valley long held by the Ichma, the Wari and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>. It became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> and then of the republic, and it is now a city of about 10 million people.</em></p>

